      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to compute the sum of the floor values of the square roots from $\\sqrt{1}$ to $\\sqrt{16}$. This can be done by evaluating $\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor$ for each integer $n$ from 1 to 16 and then summing these values.\n\nWe can list out the values:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor = 1, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{7} \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{8} \\rfloor = 2, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{9} \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{10} \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{11} \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{12} \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{13} \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{14} \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{15} \\rfloor = 3, \\\\\n&\\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor = 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nNow, we sum these values:\n\\[\n1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 4.\n\\]\n\nWe can group the terms to make the addition easier:\n\\[\n(1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2) + (3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3) + 4.\n\\]\n\nEach group sum is:\n\\[\n1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 = 12,\n\\]\n\\[\n3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 = 21,\n\\]\nand there are three terms of 4. So, we add these together:\n\\[\n12 + 21 + 4 \\times 3 = 12 + 21 + 12 = 45.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{45}.\n\\]",
