      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To solve the given problem, we need to compute the sum of the floor values of the square roots of the integers from 1 to 16. The steps involved are as follows:\n\n1. Identify the range of \\(\\sqrt{n}\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 16.\n2. Calculate \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor\\) for each \\(n\\) in this range.\n3. Sum these floor values.\n\nLet's proceed with the calculations:\n\n1. **Determine the range of \\(\\sqrt{n}\\) for \\(n\\) from 1 to 16:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{1} \\leq \\sqrt{n} \\leq \\sqrt{16}\n   \\]\n\n   Therefore,\n\n   \\[\n   1 \\leq \\sqrt{n} \\leq 4\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor\\) for each \\(n\\) from 1 to 16:**\n\n   - For \\(1 \\leq n \\leq 3\\), \\(\\sqrt{n}\\) is between 1 and 2, so \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor = 1\\).\n   - For \\(4 \\leq n \\leq 8\\), \\(\\sqrt{n}\\) is between 2 and 3, so \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor = 2\\).\n   - For \\(9 \\leq n \\leq 15\\), \\(\\sqrt{n}\\) is between 3 and 4, so \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{n} \\rfloor = 3\\).\n   - For \\(n = 16\\), \\(\\sqrt{16} = 4\\), so \\(\\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor = 4\\).\n\n3. **Sum these floor values:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\lfloor \\sqrt{1} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{2} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{3} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{4} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{5} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{6} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{7} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{8} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{9} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{10} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{11} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{12} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{13} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{14} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{15} \\rfloor + \\lfloor \\sqrt{16} \\rfloor\n   \\]\n\n   Breaking it down:\n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   &1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 2 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 3 + 4 \\\\\n   &= (3 \\times 1) + (3 \\times 2) + (3 \\times 3) + (3 \\times 4) + (1 \\times 4) \\\\\n   &= 3 + 6 + 9 + 12 + 4 \\\\\n   &= 32\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is \\(\\boxed{32}\\).",
